INT. BOMBED CONVENT HOSPITAL - CELLAR - NIGHT

1944. A low stone cellar. Candle stubs. A cot, a bucket, a stack of crates marked with red crosses. Plaster dust rains gently with each distant explosion. A water pipe drips in the dark.

NURSE ODETTE BRANDT, 30s, apron torn at the hem, bandages wrapped around her wrists like bracelets, kneels on the flagstones sorting what little remains of the pharmacy. Across from her, wedged against a barrel, sits CAPTAIN ZELDA MOSS, 30s, in an officer's tunic stiff with dried mud, one boot off, one leg propped and bound with a splint made from a broom handle.

Nobody has spoken for a long time.

ODETTE
I have four morphine ampoules left.

ZELDA
Save them.

ODETTE
For whom?

ZELDA
For the ones who can still be saved.

ODETTE
She asked for water an hour ago. And she hasn't said anything since.

ZELDA
Who?

ODETTE
The girl on the third cot.

ZELDA
I'm not talking about her.

ODETTE
I know.

ZELDA
Then why bring her up?

ODETTE
Because she is the only one who is still here to be saved.

Zelda's mouth twitches. She looks at the splinted leg.

ZELDA
Is it broken?

ODETTE
The bone is cracked. Not through.

ZELDA
So I can walk.

ODETTE
She can walk.

ZELDA
I said "I."

ODETTE
Sorry. I meant you.

A shell lands close. Dust falls from the vault in a long, soft sheet. The candles gutter. Odette covers the crates with her body. Zelda puts her hand over her eyes. The shudder passes.

ZELDA
That was ours.

ODETTE
How can you tell?

ZELDA
The whistle goes down at the end.

ODETTE
What does that mean?

ZELDA
It means somebody on our side thinks we are already dead.

ODETTE
Then they will walk the line tomorrow.

ZELDA
If there is a tomorrow.

ODETTE
There will be.

ZELDA
You say that to every patient.

ODETTE
I say it to myself.

Zelda laughs. A short, bitter sound. She lets her head fall back against the barrel.

ZELDA
Do you have a family?

ODETTE
A sister, in Lyon.

ZELDA
I had a brother in Leeds.

ODETTE
Had?

ZELDA
Have. I think.

Silence. The pipe drips. Odette picks up a vial. Reads the label. Puts it down.

ODETTE
I'm going to give you one.

ZELDA
No.

ODETTE
Captain.

ZELDA
I said no, Nurse.

ODETTE
She said no. Is that right?

ZELDA
She means me.

ODETTE
Then I'll obey her.

Zelda snorts. Odette stays where she is. A long beat. Above them, boots on the broken floor. Voices. A rifle butt knocks the cellar door.

Both women look up. Zelda reaches for a pistol on the crate. Odette puts her hand over Zelda's.

ODETTE (CONT'D)
Wait.

ZELDA
Wait for what?

ODETTE
For a word.

The door bursts. A man's voice in English, loud and cracking.

ZELDA
(a whisper)
That's one of ours.

ODETTE
Then say it.

Zelda draws breath, gathers herself, and shouts up the stairs in a hoarse, cracked voice.

ZELDA
Down here! We are down here!

The boots thunder toward them. Odette picks up the ampoule and, very slowly, tucks it back in the crate. Then she begins to cry, softly, without sound.
